 ## Report: Test creator cohorts first, then sell to employers
 
-Aurelia's most promising B2B2C route is **creator-led cohorts, but not at the assumptions in Eugene's Retreat-in-a-Box spec**. Creators bring buyers at close to zero media cost to Aurelia, and that matters because paid acquisition for a wellness subscription costs roughly $100–$400 per payer against about $36 of median first-year payer revenue in Health & Fitness. The spec's headline numbers do not survive contact with the evidence, though. **76.5% still active at week 8 is about 16 times the measured day-30 retention of mindfulness apps (4.7%)**, a 60/40 split is far richer for the platform than the 0–10% creators pay on Kajabi, Maven or Teachable, and **Aurelia's 40% of a €19.99 web ticket comes to about €6.45, which premium AI voice generation for 24 sessions can wipe out on its own**. Employers are the second real channel. A content-only app sits at about $50–70 per seat per year (Wysa, Calm Teams), not at the "double digits per member per month" that clinical-care vendors charge, and buyers now judge renewal on activation dashboards. Insurers and health systems are closed to Aurelia until it has clinical evidence, and pursuing that evidence would turn Aurelia into a regulated medical device. Of the partner channels, the evidence favours deals where the end user enters their own card: trial codes that renew at retail, carrier billing, and wearable data integrations. Free placements on airline seatbacks and hotel TVs mostly lapsed. Underneath every channel sits a compliance floor that is not optional. The spec's "+85.7% sleep improvement" and "stress −50%" are invented figures and cannot be used in any market, creators' social posts can turn the product into a medical device under UK guidance, and a free-text "how I feel" input guarantees crisis disclosures. The first test should be a small, instrumented, digital-only cohort pilot with three to five creators, judged on fill rate, week-by-week activity and Plus conversion per ticket buyer rather than per graduate.
+Aurelia's most promising B2B2C route is **creator-led cohorts, but not at the assumptions in the Retreat-in-a-Box spec**. Creators bring buyers at close to zero media cost to Aurelia, and that matters because paid acquisition for a wellness subscription costs roughly $100–$400 per payer against about $36 of median first-year payer revenue in Health & Fitness. The spec's headline numbers do not survive contact with the evidence, though. **76.5% still active at week 8 is about 16 times the measured day-30 retention of mindfulness apps (4.7%)**, a 60/40 split is far richer for the platform than the 0–10% creators pay on Kajabi, Maven or Teachable, and **Aurelia's 40% of a €19.99 web ticket comes to about €6.45, which premium AI voice generation for 24 sessions can wipe out on its own**. Employers are the second real channel. A content-only app sits at about $50–70 per seat per year (Wysa, Calm Teams), not at the "double digits per member per month" that clinical-care vendors charge, and buyers now judge renewal on activation dashboards. Insurers and health systems are closed to Aurelia until it has clinical evidence, and pursuing that evidence would turn Aurelia into a regulated medical device. Of the partner channels, the evidence favours deals where the end user enters their own card: trial codes that renew at retail, carrier billing, and wearable data integrations. Free placements on airline seatbacks and hotel TVs mostly lapsed. Underneath every channel sits a compliance floor that is not optional. The spec's "+85.7% sleep improvement" and "stress −50%" are invented figures and cannot be used in any market, creators' social posts can turn the product into a medical device under UK guidance, and a free-text "how I feel" input guarantees crisis disclosures. The first test should be a small, instrumented, digital-only cohort pilot with three to five creators, judged on fill rate, week-by-week activity and Plus conversion per ticket buyer rather than per graduate.
 
